{%- doc -%}
  A single frame on the contact sheet: one image with a small reference number printed in
  the corner, the way a lab prints the frame number along the edge of a proof.

  This block is specific to the Contact Sheet section. It will render anywhere a theme
  block is accepted, but the numbered corner and the film border only make sense inside a
  sheet, so it is not intended for general reuse.

  Three settings shape the frame itself. image_ratio decides whether the window is square,
  upright or landscape, which matters because a roll shot in portrait orientation should
  proof as portrait rather than being cropped square. frame_number prints the reference,
  left as free text rather than a counter because photographers skip and reshoot frames, so
  the numbers on a real sheet are rarely a clean sequence. product links the frame to the
  item in the catalogue that this shot documents.

  That product link is what makes the structured content work. The Contact Sheet lives on
  the homepage, where there is no product in context, so closest.product would always come
  back empty. Picking the product on the block instead means the metafield and metaobject
  data can be read wherever the sheet is placed.

  Two pieces of structured content render underneath the frame.

  The shot data metafield, custom.shot_data, is a single line of text holding the exposure
  settings for that frame. It prints as a monospaced line under the number badge.

  The film stock reference, custom.shot_on, is a list of film_stock metaobject entries. Each
  entry carries the stock name, the process, the ISO it was made for and a description of
  its grain. Because it is a list rather than a single reference, one frame can credit more
  than one stock, which happens when a shot is duplicated across two rolls for comparison.

  Blank state: if the product is not set, or the metafield is empty, or no stock is
  referenced, the strip does not render at all. No wrapper, no border, no heading with
  nothing beneath it. Each of the three pieces is guarded separately, so a frame with shot
  data but no stock reference shows the data line alone rather than an empty stock list
  beside it.
{%- enddoc -%}

{% assign block_settings = block.settings %}

{% liquid
  # The product this frame documents. Chosen on the block rather than inherited from the
  # page, because the Contact Sheet sits on the homepage where no product is in context.
  assign frame_product = block_settings.product

  # Single line text metafield holding the exposure settings for this frame.
  assign shot_data = frame_product.metafields.custom.shot_data

  # List of film_stock metaobject entries. The .value is what turns the reference into
  # the entries themselves rather than a list of ids.
  assign film_stocks = frame_product.metafields.custom.shot_on.value

  # Alt text falls back to the frame number so the image is never announced as unlabelled
  # to a screen reader, even when the merchant has not written a description.
  assign frame_alt = block_settings.image.alt | default: block_settings.frame_number

  # Worked out up front so the markup below can decide in one test whether the strip is
  # needed at all, rather than opening a wrapper and then discovering it has no contents.
  assign has_structured_content = false
  if shot_data != blank or film_stocks != blank
    assign has_structured_content = true
  endif
%}

<figure
  class="contact-frame contact-frame--ratio-{{ block_settings.image_ratio }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div class="contact-frame__window">
    {% if block_settings.image != blank %}
      {{
        block_settings.image
        | image_url: width: 600
        | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', class: 'contact-frame__image', alt: frame_alt
      }}
    {% else %}
      {% comment %}
        Placeholder keeps the grid from collapsing while a merchant is still choosing
        images in the editor.
      {% endcomment %}
      {{ 'image' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'contact-frame__placeholder' }}
    {% endif %}
  </div>

  {% if block_settings.frame_number != blank %}
    <figcaption class="contact-frame__number">{{ block_settings.frame_number }}</figcaption>
  {% endif %}

  {% comment %}
    The structured content strip. The outer test means that when a frame has no product
    linked, or the linked product carries neither piece of data, nothing is written at
    all: no element, no border, no vertical space.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% if has_structured_content %}
    <div class="contact-frame__data">
      {% if shot_data != blank %}
        {% comment %}
          metafield_text is correct here rather than metafield_tag, because the value is a
          single line of plain text and metafield_tag would wrap it in markup this layout
          does not want.
        {% endcomment %}
        <p class="contact-frame__shot">{{ shot_data | metafield_text }}</p>
      {% endif %}

      {% if film_stocks != blank %}
        <ul class="contact-frame__stocks">
          {% for stock in film_stocks %}
            <li class="contact-frame__stock">
              <span class="contact-frame__stock-name">{{ stock.stock_name.value }}</span>

              {% comment %}
                Process and ISO are printed on one line as a spec pair. Each is guarded
                separately, because an entry can legitimately carry one without the other:
                an instant film has a process but no meaningful rated ISO.
              {% endcomment %}
              {% if stock.process.value != blank or stock.native_iso.value != blank %}
                <span class="contact-frame__stock-spec">
                  {%- if stock.process.value != blank -%}
                    {{ stock.process.value }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  {%- if stock.process.value != blank and stock.native_iso.value != blank -%}
                    {{ ' · ' }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  {%- if stock.native_iso.value != blank -%}
                    ISO {{ stock.native_iso.value }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                </span>
              {% endif %}

              {% if stock.grain_character.value != blank %}
                <span class="contact-frame__stock-grain">{{ stock.grain_character.value }}</span>
              {% endif %}
            </li>
          {% endfor %}
        </ul>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</figure>

{% stylesheet %}
  .contact-frame {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .contact-frame__window {
    overflow: hidden;
    background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35);
    border: 1px solid var(--contact-sheet-line, rgb(128 128 128 / 0.3));
  }

  /* Ratio variants. Each sets one property, but as a class rather than a variable
     because the values are fixed rather than merchant-entered. */
  .contact-frame--ratio-square .contact-frame__window {
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
  }

  .contact-frame--ratio-portrait .contact-frame__window {
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 4;
  }

  .contact-frame--ratio-landscape .contact-frame__window {
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
  }

  .contact-frame__image,
  .contact-frame__placeholder {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .contact-frame__placeholder {
    opacity: 0.25;
  }

  /* The reference number, sat in the corner of the window in the same monospaced face as
     the sheet label so the two read as part of the same printed object. */
  .contact-frame__number {
    position: absolute;
    top: 6px;
    left: 8px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, 'SFMono-Regular', 'Menlo', monospace;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.55);
    color: #ffcf8f;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* The structured content strip. This whole block only exists in the markup when there
     is something to put in it, so there is no empty-state styling to write. */
  .contact-frame__data {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, 'SFMono-Regular', 'Menlo', monospace;
  }

  .contact-frame__shot {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .contact-frame__stocks {
    margin: 6px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .contact-frame__stock {
    padding-left: 8px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--contact-sheet-line, rgb(128 128 128 / 0.3));
  }

  .contact-frame__stock + .contact-frame__stock {
    margin-top: 6px;
  }

  .contact-frame__stock-name,
  .contact-frame__stock-spec,
  .contact-frame__stock-grain {
    display: block;
  }

  .contact-frame__stock-name {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .contact-frame__stock-spec {
    font-size: 0.625rem;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .contact-frame__stock-grain {
    margin-top: 3px;
    font-size: 0.625rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    opacity: 0.6;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:names.contact_frame",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image",
      "label": "t:settings.image"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "frame_number",
      "label": "t:settings.frame_number",
      "default": "14A",
      "info": "t:info.frame_number"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "image_ratio",
      "label": "t:settings.image_ratio",
      "options": [
        {
          "value": "square",
          "label": "t:options.square"
        },
        {
          "value": "portrait",
          "label": "t:options.portrait"
        },
        {
          "value": "landscape",
          "label": "t:options.landscape"
        }
      ],
      "default": "square"
    },
    {
      "type": "product",
      "id": "product",
      "label": "t:settings.product",
      "info": "t:info.frame_product"
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "t:names.contact_frame",
      "category": "t:categories.storytelling"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
